        "howNativesThink": "Native speakers evaluate bread and pizza quality primarily by the crust: a great sourdough has a dark, crackling, chewy crust ('great crust'), while a good pizza requires a blistered, airy crust. In contrast, in everyday home or school contexts, children notoriously dislike sandwich crusts ('cut off the crusts'). In fine catering and corporate afternoon teas, removing bread crusts to make delicate finger sandwiches denotes elegance and refinement.",
        "mentalImage": "A rustic wooden peel sliding a round sourdough loaf from a stone deck oven, revealing an intricately scored, mahogany-brown blistered crust dusted with white flour that emits an audible crackling sound as it begins to cool on a wire rack.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with 'crumb' (the soft inner portion of bread), 'dough' (unbaked mixture), or 'frosting/icing'. Do not use as a slang substitute for money in modern professional English (though 'crust' historically meant livelihood in archaic idioms like 'earn a crust').",
