        "culturalContext": "Passing hors-d'oeuvres is the gold standard of networking hospitality in English-speaking corporate culture. In TOEIC Parts 3, 4, and 7, dialogues and event schedules frequently reference 'cocktails and hors d'oeuvres' as the opening segment of an annual shareholders' gala, retirement dinner, or product launch reception.",
        "howNativesThink": "To native speakers, 'hors-d'oeuvre' denotes upscale social entertaining and polished corporate hospitality. Unlike casual 'snacks' (chips, pretzels) or a seated 'first course / starter' (such as a bowl of soup at a dining table), hors-d'oeuvres are inherently social and mobile: guests hold a drink in one hand while taking a delicate canap\u00e9, smoked salmon blini, or prosciutto-wrapped melon skewer with a cocktail napkin in the other. In event planning and TOEIC reading passages, caterers specify whether hors-d'oeuvres are 'passed' (circulated by servers) or 'stationed' (set on display tables).",
