          "mentalImage": "A glass ramekin of vibrant ruby-red raspberry jam with visible fruit seeds sitting on a white porcelain breakfast tray next to a warm, golden-brown toasted croissant and a butter knife.",
          "whenToUse": "Use when discussing fruit spreads, breakfast condiment selections, hotel buffet amenities, bakery fillings (e.g., jam-filled donuts or tarts), or pantry grocery items.",
          "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with 'jelly' (made from strained clear juice with gelatin/pectin, without pulp or seeds), 'marmalade' (specifically citrus-based with rind), or 'chutney' (savory/spiced fruit preserve cooked with vinegar and spices). Outside dining, avoid using it when referring to mechanical stoppages unless clear context (like 'paper jam') is intended.",
          "culturalContext": "In traditional British afternoon tea, the order of spreading clotted cream and jam on a scone is a famous cultural debate (Devon method spreads cream first, then jam; Cornish method spreads jam first, then cream). In corporate hotel catering, providing individual miniature jars of branded fruit jam (e.g., Bonne Maman) signals high quality on breakfast trays."
