        "evolution": "In medieval England, pastry enclosures were known as 'coffins' or 'coffers', functioning initially as thick, inedible vessels of flour and water designed to braise, preserve, and transport meats, game, and suet over long periods. The vernacular term 'pie' appeared in Anglo-Latin and Middle English during the early 14th century to describe baked pasties containing assorted meats and spices. By the 16th century, as enriched fats like butter and lard were incorporated into the dough, the pastry casing became tender, flaky, and edible. While the British tradition maintained an enduring focus on savory meat pies (steak and ale, pork pie, shepherd's pie), American colonial bakers adapted the technique to abundant native and orchard fruits (apples, pumpkins, berries, pecans), cementing sweet fruit pies baked in sloping metal tins as an enduring symbol of American culinary culture.",
