        "firstRecordedUse": "Early 17th century (c. 1610s in philosophical and scientific texts describing the physics of chilling)",
        "origin": "From Latin \"refriger\u0101ti\u014d\" (a cooling, refreshing, relief from heat), noun of action formed from the past-participle stem of \"refriger\u0101re\" (to cool off, make cool), compounded from \"re-\" (intensive prefix or back/again) + \"fr\u012bger\u0101re\" (to make cold), derived from \"fr\u012bgus\" (cold, coldness, frost), from Proto-Indo-European \"*sriH-g-\" / \"*srig-\" (cold, frost).",
