        "howNativesThink": "To native speakers, particularly in British, Commonwealth, and American culinary parlance, a 'saucepan' is the standard go-to small-to-medium pot found in every household and commercial galley. While Americans sometimes casually refer to smaller pots generically as 'pots', 'saucepan' specifically evokes the single long handle and high vertical walls (as opposed to a frying pan/skillet which has low sloped sides, or a stockpot which is huge with two loop handles). In restaurant operations, it signifies precision heating\u2014delicate roux, velvet reductions, boiled eggs, or warmed glazes.",
        "mentalImage": "A heavy-gauge stainless steel saucepan resting on an industrial gas burner, steam curling off a simmering reduction sauce as a line cook with folded kitchen towels whisks vigorously while gripping the long tubular handle.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not use for shallow pans with flared sides meant for frying or searing (use \"frying pan\", \"skillet\", or \"saut\u00e9 pan\"); do not use for very large two-handled vessels used for simmering stocks or boiling pasta in bulk (use \"stockpot\" or \"Dutch oven\").",
        "whenToUse": "Use when referring specifically to a deep cooking pot with a flat base, tall straight or slightly rounded sides, and one long handle used for boiling liquids, making gravies, simmering grains, or heating soups."
