        "commonMistakes": "Learners often place primary stress on the first syllable (saying /\u02c8s\u0254\u02d0te\u026a/ or /\u02c8sa\u028ate\u026a/) instead of the correct French-derived final-syllable stress (/so\u028a\u02c8te\u026a/). Non-native speakers also mistakenly assume the final 'e' is silent as in common English words like 'bite' or 'taste', pronouncing it as a single syllable (/so\u028at/). When used as a noun, learners frequently confuse 'a saut\u00e9' (the finished dish or cooking method) with a 'saut\u00e9 pan' (the cooking vessel) or fail to realize that while 'saute' is most commonly encountered as a verb, it functions as a count noun when denoting a specific saut\u00e9ed preparation (e.g., 'a mushroom saut\u00e9').",
        "ipa": "/so\u028a\u02c8te\u026a/",
